---
/**
 * Compare — Raven vs Vulture
 * Primary keyword: raven vs vulture
 * Secondary: difference between raven and vulture, raven vs buzzard, do ravens and vultures compete
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Raven vs Vulture: Identification, Size, and Ecological Roles';
const description = 'Ravens and vultures share carcasses — but they are very different birds. This comparison covers how to tell a raven from a vulture in flight, their size differences, ecological roles, and how they interact at a carcass.';
const path        = '/compare/raven-vs-vulture/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',    url: '/' },
  { name: 'Compare', url: '/compare/' },
  { name: 'Raven vs Vulture', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How do you tell a raven from a vulture in flight?', a: 'Ravens and vultures are both large black soaring birds, which can cause confusion at distance — but they are very different on closer inspection. Key differences in flight: Turkey Vultures (North America) soar with wings in a V-shape (dihedral), rocking side to side; ravens soar with flat wings. Vultures have tiny, naked heads (usually red in Turkey Vulture); ravens have a large head with a heavy bill clearly visible in silhouette. Vultures are substantially larger (Turkey Vulture wingspan 170–183 cm vs raven\'s 115–150 cm). In Europe, Eurasian griffon vultures dwarf ravens; Black Vultures are more similarly sized but show very different wing shape and proportions.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens compete with vultures at carcasses?', a: 'Yes — ravens and vultures are both scavengers and regularly compete at large carcasses. At most carcasses the outcome depends on relative numbers: a large group of griffon vultures or turkey vultures will displace ravens; solitary or small groups of vultures may be driven away by ravens mobbing in groups. Ravens arrive at carcasses faster than most vultures (better distributed visual scanning; following other animals to carcasses) and may consume significant portions before vultures arrive. Once vultures arrive in numbers, ravens defer and pick at the edges.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens related to vultures?', a: 'No — ravens and vultures are not closely related. Ravens are passerine birds (order Passeriformes), in the family Corvidae. New World vultures (Turkey Vulture, Condors) are in the order Accipitriformes or Cathartiformes (closely related to storks — their resemblance to Old World vultures is convergent evolution). Old World vultures (griffon, bearded, black vultures of Europe/Africa/Asia) are in the order Accipitriformes (with hawks and eagles). Ravens and vultures share black plumage and scavenging behaviour as convergent adaptations, not ancestry.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-08" dateModified="2026-11-08"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="qa-page">

  <nav class="qa-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
